{"name":"Angry Hornet","hex":"#ee9911","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"angry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Displaying or feeling anger.","Inflamed and painful."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An angry person."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/angry"},{"term":"hornet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any large wasp of the genus Vespa, having a brown-and-yellow-striped body and the ability to inflict a serious sting.","A person who pesters with petty but ceaseless attacks."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hornet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hornet","blocks":[{"text":"Hornets are wasps of the genus Vespa in the subfamily Vespinae. They are the largest of the eusocial wasps. Species like the European hornet can grow to 35 mm (1.4 in) in length, while other species like the Asian giant hornet can exceed 50 mm (2.0 in). They are similar in appearance to their close relatives, the yellowjackets, but are distinguished from other vespine wasps by the relatively large top margin of the head, or vertex. Worldwide, 22 species of Vespa are recognized. Most species only occur in the tropics of Asia, though the European hornet is widely distributed throughout Europe, Russia, North America, and north-eastern Asia. Wasps of the genus Dolichovespula native to North America are commonly called hornets, but are actually yellowjackets."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Hornet.jpg/330px-Hornet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hornet"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Hornet.jpg/500px-Hornet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Hornet.jpg/500px-Hornet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link"